<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marie Antoinette</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1755">1755</a>–<a href="/years/1793">1793</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg-lorraine">House of Habsburg-Lorraine</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marie Antoinette (1755–1793) was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>. A daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, she married the future king in 1770 to seal the Franco-Austrian alliance and became a target of popular hostility for her extravagance and opposition to reform. After the fall of the monarchy she was tried by the Revolutionary Tribunal and guillotined on 16 October 1793.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette.jpg" alt="Marie Antoinette" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Élisabeth Louise Vigée Le Brun · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louise_Elisabeth_Vig%C3%A9e-Lebrun_-_Marie-Antoinette_dit_%C2%AB_%C3%A0_la_Rose_%C2%BB_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maria Antonia Josepha Johanna was born on 2 November 1755 at the Hofburg Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, the fifteenth child and youngest daughter of Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-holy-roman-emperor">Francis I, Holy Roman Emperor</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, Maria Theresa ended her long hostility with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Both wished to check the ambitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, and they sealed the alliance with a marriage: on 7 February 1770 Louis XV formally asked for her hand for his grandson and heir, the Dauphin. When Louis XV died on 10 May 1774, the dauphin became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> and she became queen consort. Two weeks later the king gave her the Petit Trianon, a small château at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As queen she became a target of those who opposed the policies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> and of the monarchy in general. Libelles accused her of extravagance, promiscuity and sympathy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. In 1785 members of her court defrauded jewellers in the Affair of the Diamond Necklace; she was falsely accused, but her reputation suffered. She was later called Madame Déficit, as the financial crisis was blamed on her spending and her opposition to reforms proposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-robert-jacques-turgot">Turgot</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-necker">Necker</a>. In July 1789 she urged Necker's dismissal, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> rose to storm the Bastille.</p><p>In June 1791 the royal family tried to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montmedy">Montmédy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flight-to-varennes">Flight to Varennes</a>, which failed through poor execution. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> on 20 April 1792, and the queen was seen as an enemy. The attack on the Tuileries on 10 August 1792 forced the family to take refuge at the Legislative Assembly, and they were imprisoned in the Temple. The monarchy was abolished on 21 September, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> was executed on 21 January 1793.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Marie Antoinette was moved to the Conciergerie, and a plot to free her failed in late August. Her trial before the Revolutionary Tribunal began on 14 October 1793; she and her lawyers had less than a day to prepare. The charges included sending millions of livres to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and incest, an accusation made by her son Louis-Charles under pressure from Jacques Hébert. She was convicted of high treason and guillotined on 16 October 1793. Her remains were exhumed in 1815, during the Bourbon Restoration, and buried at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-saint-denis">Basilica of Saint-Denis</a>. For many revolutionaries she symbolised the faults of the old regime.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marie Antoinette” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ferdinand IV, who succeeded in 1759 when his father Charles left to become king of Spain, and his wife Maria Carolina of Austria, sister of <b>Marie Antoinette</b>, turned against reformers after the French Revolution.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The tune was a popular contredanse, &quot;Le carillon national&quot;, by Jean-Antoine Bécourt, a musician of the Théâtre Beaujolais; Queen <b>Marie Antoinette</b> is said to have often played it on her harpsichord.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ca-ira">Ça Ira</a></li><li><blockquote>It was sung mainly as a rallying cry and an entertainment of the revolutionaries, and it mocked King Louis XVI, Queen <b>Marie Antoinette</b> and the monarchists.</blockquote><a href="/songs/carmagnole">La Carmagnole</a></li><li><blockquote>When Prince Louis-Joseph, son of Louis XVI and <b>Marie Antoinette</b> and heir to the throne, was born in 1781, his wet nurse, a peasant named Geneviève Poitrine, sang it as she rocked the royal cradle.</blockquote><a href="/songs/malbrough-sen-va-t-en-guerre">Malbrough s'en va-t-en guerre</a></li><li><blockquote>After a two-day trial before the Revolutionary Tribunal, the former queen <b>Marie Antoinette</b> was executed on the Place de la Révolution.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-16">16 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hofburg Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in place de la Concorde, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1779" aria-label="Hofburg Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4930 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Marie Antoinette"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5380 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5380 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1779">What was here in 1779? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/francis-i-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Francis I, Holy Roman Emperor</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; 1708–1765</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/maria-theresa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Maria Theresa</span><span class="tp">Mother &#183; 1717–1780</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-xvi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Louis XVI</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; 1754–1793</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…you were not wished for, but you are not on that account less dear to me. A son would have been rather the property of the State.&#8221;</p><footer>Words to her newborn daughter, 1778 &#183; English translation in the Court Memoirs of France series (translator not named) &#183; as recorded by her lady-in-waiting, Madame Campan &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3891/pg3891-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…if the poor whom you have succoured for the past twenty years had each placed a single candle in their windows it would have been the most beautiful illumination ever witnessed.&#8221;</p><footer>Words to a surgeon of Versailles &#183; English translation in the Court Memoirs of France series (translator not named) &#183; as recorded by her lady-in-waiting, Madame Campan &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3891/pg3891-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
